# MECHANISM KDF-EMV-MASTER-A&#xD;
&#xD;
## Purpose&#xD;
&#xD;
Host-side EMV ICC Master Key Derivation Option A, for personalization and issuer processing.&#xD;
Derives a card-specific ICC Master Key from an Issuer Master Key (IMK), the complete PAN&#xD;
including its check digit, and the PAN Sequence Number (PSN). This does not calculate a cryptogram.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Conformance Status&#xD;
&#xD;
The normative implementation review and formula comparison against EMV Contact Book 2 v4.4&#xD;
were completed on 13 October 2026 for commit `881ec9219ca7f558603e98acb3696b7e5219201e`.&#xD;
The review covered A1.3.1 (pages 137-138), A1.4.1 (pages 138-139), and A1.4.2 (page 139).&#xD;
This mechanism's construction matches A1.4.1; no security- or interoperability-relevant&#xD;
normative deviation was found. This is a normative implementation review, not EMV product certification&#xD;
or an official EMVCo approval. Fixed comparison and characterization values are not certification evidence.&#xD;
&#xD;
The review does not establish complete Application Cryptogram or ARPC processing, including&#xD;
the eight-byte AES Application Cryptogram integration, or Secure Messaging.&#xD;
Linux/Unix and FIPS runtime verification remain outside the demonstrated scope;&#xD;
Windows test results are not cross-platform evidence.&#xD;
The provisional PAN limit of 1-19 digits is a technical decision,&#xD;
not a normative conformity assertion. Option B is available separately as KDF-EMV-MASTER-B;&#xD;
it is never selected automatically by Option A.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input Contract&#xD;
&#xD;
The IMK must be a genuine KEY reference containing exactly 16 bytes (K1 || K2); expected metadata is Secret TDEA.&#xD;
DES3 parity and weak/degenerate-key findings are non-blocking warnings; conflicting type or DeriveKey usage also emits a warning.&#xD;
The IMK is not adjusted for parity or modified. Key literals and non-KEY variables are rejected.&#xD;
&#xD;
PAN is the third Derive argument: a direct normal string literal or a referenced normal string&#xD;
VAR (not a HEX/Base64 VAR). It contains 1-19 ASCII digits, retaining leading zeros.&#xD;
Nested function results, integers, HEX/Base64 literals, KEY/PARAM references and implicit&#xD;
conversions are rejected. No trimming, normalization, Luhn check or check-digit removal occurs.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameter Contract&#xD;
&#xD;
Parameters contain exactly one consistent MECH and optionally one PSN; no other named parameters.&#xD;
PSN is supplied only as a normal string literal containing exactly two ASCII digits, e.g.&#xD;
`#PSN:"45"`. Omission defaults to `"00"`; empty, one-digit, integer, variable, HEX and Base64&#xD;
values are rejected. Duplicate PSN and MECH declarations are rejected even if identical;&#xD;
stored MECH must agree with the selected mechanism. These rules also apply to direct PARAM&#xD;
declarations and serialized legacy parameter sets.&#xD;
Serialized segments without a colon or value are rejected before legacy parsing; a malformed&#xD;
PSN never selects the default. PSN is forbidden for mechanisms whose registry contract does&#xD;
not explicitly support it, including AES-CBC, DES3-ECB and KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Derivation Algorithm&#xD;
&#xD;
1. X = PAN || PSN as decimal digits.&#xD;
2. D = the rightmost 16 digits of X, or left-pad with zero digits to 16 digits.&#xD;
3. Pack D into eight bytes (two decimal nibbles per byte); F1 = this block.&#xD;
4. F2 = F1 XOR FF FF FF FF FF FF FF FF, the full bitwise complement.&#xD;
5. L = TDEA-ECB(IMK, F1); R = TDEA-ECB(IMK, F2). No padding or IV is used.&#xD;
6. ICC_RAW = L || R, exactly 16 bytes.&#xD;
7. Set DES Odd Parity on ICC_RAW, changing only bit 0 of each byte.&#xD;
&#xD;
Unlike this master-key derivation, KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION does not adjust its output parity.&#xD;
No result repair, retry, extra transformation or automatic Option B fallback is performed.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
```text&#xD;
KEY IMK = GenerateKey(DES3-ECB, 0x(0123456789ABCDEFFEDCBA9876543210))&#xD;
PARAM p = Parameters(KDF-EMV-MASTER-A, #PSN:"45")&#xD;
KEY ICC = Derive(p, IMK, "99012345678901234")&#xD;
PARAM sessionParameters = Parameters(KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION)&#xD;
KEY sessionKey = Derive(sessionParameters, ICC, 0x(0001))&#xD;
```&#xD;
&#xD;
ICC = 67F8292358083E5EA7AB7FDA58D53B6B.&#xD;
Session key = 68533955B11DEC088A36B4871DEAF9CF (no session-key parity adjustment).&#xD;
&#xD;
An equivalent direct declaration is `PARAM p = #MECH:KDF-EMV-MASTER-A #PSN:"45"`.&#xD;
For the default PSN, use `PARAM p = Parameters(KDF-EMV-MASTER-A)`.&#xD;
PAN may also be declared with `VAR pan = "99012345678901234"` and passed as `Derive(p, IMK, pan)`.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Result KEY Metadata&#xD;
&#xD;
Algorithm TDEA; Secret material; KeySizeInBits 128 including parity bits; ValueFormat HEX;&#xD;
KeyValue and Value contain the same derived key; DerivationMechanism KDF-EMV-MASTER-A;&#xD;
KeyUsage Unspecified. The result can be passed directly to KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Security and Errors&#xD;
&#xD;
Invalid key lengths, contradictory size metadata, malformed inputs, duplicate/extra&#xD;
parameters and forbidden argument origins are rejected. Core errors do not include key,&#xD;
PAN or PSN values. Temporary key and ciphertext buffers are zeroized in finally blocks;&#xD;
the returned key remains available to the caller. Immutable CryptoScript KEY strings cannot&#xD;
be reliably zeroized. Fault-injection observation of provider exceptions and private buffer&#xD;
cleanup remains a test limitation; no special crypto abstraction was added for that test.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Reference&#xD;
&#xD;
EMV Contact Book 2 v4.4, Annex A1.4.1 Option A, pages 138-139 (formula review completed 13 October 2026).&#xD;
Published comparison values: PyEMV 1.3.0 and PyEMV kd.py (EMV 4.3 reference).&#xD;
V3 in the core tests is an independent characterization, not a normative published vector.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: Tdea. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The selected Option A/B/C determines the algorithm; conflicting KEY declarations never select another option. PAN, PSN, genuine-reference and known-size consistency checks remain binding. DES parity/weakness is diagnosed without blocking technically valid TDEA test keys. The explicitly enabled analysis backend uses the supplied effective key bits.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
### TDEA test and analysis keys&#xD;
&#xD;
`DesParityViolation` (10) and `WeakDes3Key` (11) are non-blocking Warning diagnostics. Weak or semi-weak DES components and effective K1=K2 or K2=K3 are reported independently of parity; K1=K3 alone is not single-DES degeneration. Each quality category is reported at most once per operation on the original KEY, after any KeyTypeConflict and KeyUsageConflict metadata warnings. No KEY bytes or parity bits are changed. Technically valid calls also work without a diagnostic sink.&#xD;
&#xD;
This support is for test and analysis and makes no suitability claim for production payment systems. Invalid key lengths, malformed data and protocol errors remain blocking. ARQC verification and TR-31 authentication remain mandatory; failed verification stops execution without a successful result. AES behavior and the approved algorithm selectors remain unchanged.&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM KDF-EMV-MASTER-B&#xD;
&#xD;
## Purpose and Conformance Status&#xD;
&#xD;
Host-side EMV ICC Master Key Derivation Option B for personalization and issuer processing.&#xD;
Derives a card-specific ICC Master Key from an Issuer Master Key (IMK), the complete PAN&#xD;
including its check digit, and the PAN Sequence Number (PSN). It does not calculate a cryptogram.&#xD;
&#xD;
The normative implementation review and formula comparison against EMV Contact Book 2 v4.4&#xD;
were completed on 13 October 2026 for commit `881ec9219ca7f558603e98acb3696b7e5219201e`.&#xD;
The review covered A1.3.1 (pages 137-138), A1.4.1 (pages 138-139), and A1.4.2 (page 139).&#xD;
This mechanism's construction matches A1.4.2; no security- or interoperability-relevant&#xD;
normative deviation was found. This is a normative implementation review, not EMV product certification&#xD;
or an official EMVCo approval. Published comparison and characterization values are not certification evidence.&#xD;
&#xD;
The review does not establish complete Application Cryptogram or ARPC processing, including&#xD;
the eight-byte AES Application Cryptogram integration, or Secure Messaging.&#xD;
Linux/Unix and FIPS runtime verification remain outside the demonstrated scope;&#xD;
Windows test results are not cross-platform evidence.&#xD;
The PAN limit of 1-19 digits is a provisional technical&#xD;
contract, not a normative conformity assertion.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input and Parameter Contract&#xD;
&#xD;
Use exactly three Derive arguments: PARAM, a genuine KEY reference of exactly&#xD;
16 bytes, and PAN. KEY literals, non-KEY references and value-based structured KEY lookup&#xD;
are not allowed. Expected metadata is Secret TDEA with DeriveKey usage; conflicts warn.&#xD;
DES3 parity and weak/degenerate-key findings are non-blocking warnings. The input key and its parity are not changed.&#xD;
&#xD;
PAN must be a direct normal string literal or a referenced normal string VAR containing&#xD;
1-19 ASCII decimal digits. Leading zeros and the check digit are retained. HEX/Base64,&#xD;
integer, KEY/PARAM and nested-function results are rejected; there is no implicit conversion,&#xD;
trimming, normalization, Luhn validation or check-digit removal.&#xD;
&#xD;
Only MECH and optional PSN parameters are permitted. MECH must consistently select&#xD;
KDF-EMV-MASTER-B. PSN must be a direct normal string literal with exactly two ASCII digits,&#xD;
for example `#PSN:"45"`; omission defaults to `"00"`. Integer, variable, HEX/Base64 and malformed&#xD;
PSN values are rejected. Duplicate PSN or MECH declarations are rejected even if identical.&#xD;
These rules apply to Parameters(...), direct PARAM declarations and serialized legacy PARAMs.&#xD;
Malformed serialized segments are rejected before lossy legacy parsing; malformed PSN never&#xD;
selects the default. PSN remains forbidden for mechanisms not explicitly supporting it.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Derivation Algorithm&#xD;
&#xD;
The branch depends on PAN.Length alone, not PAN.Length + PSN.Length.&#xD;
&#xD;
- PAN with 1-16 digits: exactly Option-A preparation. Take the rightmost 16 digits of&#xD;
  PAN || PSN, left-pad with zero digits if shorter, then encode eight Packed-Decimal bytes as F1.&#xD;
- PAN with 17-19 digits: concatenate the full PAN and PSN. For odd PAN length prepend exactly&#xD;
  one zero digit. Encode Packed Decimal before binary SHA-1; do not hash ASCII/UTF-8 text.&#xD;
  Scan the resulting 40 nibbles high-nibble first and collect the first 16 nibbles 0-9.&#xD;
  If fewer than 16 occur, scan again for A-F, convert A-F to 0-5, and append until 16 digits&#xD;
  exist. Pack these digits into eight bytes as F1. There is no second hash or modulo-10 pass.&#xD;
&#xD;
For both branches, F2 is the complete bitwise complement of F1. Encrypt F1 and F2 using&#xD;
TDEA-ECB(IMK), without IV or padding. Concatenate the two ciphertexts in that order and&#xD;
set DES Odd Parity in-place, changing only bit 0 of each byte. The output is exactly 16 bytes.&#xD;
&#xD;
Option A always uses its rightmost-digit procedure even for long PANs. Option B selects its&#xD;
own short/long branch; it is never an automatic fallback from Option A. A short-PAN Option-B&#xD;
result still reports DerivationMechanism KDF-EMV-MASTER-B.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Executable Example&#xD;
&#xD;
```text&#xD;
KEY IMK = GenerateKey(DES3-ECB, 0x(0123456789ABCDEFFEDCBA9876543210))&#xD;
PARAM p = Parameters(KDF-EMV-MASTER-B, #PSN:"45")&#xD;
KEY ICC = Derive(p, IMK, "99012345678901234")&#xD;
PARAM sessionParameters = Parameters(KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION)&#xD;
KEY sessionKey = Derive(sessionParameters, ICC, 0x(0001))&#xD;
```&#xD;
&#xD;
ICC = 985EC4FD3EDF6162E31AF1C7D0543416.&#xD;
Session key = 43A79915202AD2C8504F42A91438B152 (no session-key parity adjustment).&#xD;
&#xD;
Equivalent direct parameters: `PARAM p = #MECH:KDF-EMV-MASTER-B #PSN:"45"`.&#xD;
Default PSN: `PARAM p = Parameters(KDF-EMV-MASTER-B)`.&#xD;
PAN may be declared as `VAR pan = "99012345678901234"` and passed as `Derive(p, IMK, pan)`.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Result Metadata and Security&#xD;
&#xD;
Secret TDEA; KeySizeInBits 128 including parity bits; ValueFormat HEX; identical Value and&#xD;
KeyValue; KeyUsage Unspecified; DerivationMechanism KDF-EMV-MASTER-B. The result is directly&#xD;
usable with KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION. No extra derived-key repair or rejection policy is applied.&#xD;
&#xD;
Errors omit key, PAN and PSN values. Owned snapshots, hash buffers and temporary ECB results&#xD;
are cleared in finally blocks. Immutable CryptoScript KEY strings cannot reliably be zeroized.&#xD;
Fault injection after the first successful ECB block remains a documented test limitation;&#xD;
no additional fault-injection architecture is introduced.&#xD;
&#xD;
## References&#xD;
&#xD;
EMV Contact Book 2 v4.4, Annex A1.4.2 Option B, page 139 (formula review completed 13 October 2026).&#xD;
V1/V2 are published PyEMV comparison values, not official EMV conformance vectors.&#xD;
V3 and the three ATC-0001 session keys are independent characterization values.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: Tdea. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The selected Option A/B/C determines the algorithm; conflicting KEY declarations never select another option. PAN, PSN, genuine-reference and known-size consistency checks remain binding. DES parity/weakness is diagnosed without blocking technically valid TDEA test keys. The explicitly enabled analysis backend uses the supplied effective key bits.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
### TDEA test and analysis keys&#xD;
&#xD;
`DesParityViolation` (10) and `WeakDes3Key` (11) are non-blocking Warning diagnostics. Weak or semi-weak DES components and effective K1=K2 or K2=K3 are reported independently of parity; K1=K3 alone is not single-DES degeneration. Each quality category is reported at most once per operation on the original KEY, after any KeyTypeConflict and KeyUsageConflict metadata warnings. No KEY bytes or parity bits are changed. Technically valid calls also work without a diagnostic sink.&#xD;
&#xD;
This support is for test and analysis and makes no suitability claim for production payment systems. Invalid key lengths, malformed data and protocol errors remain blocking. ARQC verification and TR-31 authentication remain mandatory; failed verification stops execution without a successful result. AES behavior and the approved algorithm selectors remain unchanged.&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM KDF-EMV-MASTER-C&#xD;
&#xD;
## Purpose and Conformance Status&#xD;
&#xD;
Host-side EMV ICC Master Key Derivation Option C for personalization and issuer processing.&#xD;
Derives a card-specific ICC Master Key from an AES Issuer Master Key (IMK), the complete&#xD;
PAN including its check digit, and the PAN Sequence Number (PSN). It does not calculate&#xD;
an Application Cryptogram or ARPC.&#xD;
&#xD;
The core formula was directly reviewed on 13 October 2026 against EMV Contact Book 2 v4.4,&#xD;
Annex A1.4.3, page 140, and Specification Bulletin 162 (AES Key Derivation Erratum).&#xD;
The erratum's full 16-byte inversion is already incorporated into v4.4.&#xD;
This is a normative implementation review, not EMV product certification or official EMVCo&#xD;
approval. The fixed values below are independently calculated characterization values,&#xD;
not official EMVCo conformance vectors. The new language integration remains subject to&#xD;
the concluding AP5 security and integration review.&#xD;
&#xD;
The PAN limit of 1-19 digits is a provisional CryptoScript input contract, not an explicit&#xD;
normative limit from A1.4.3. The review does not establish complete Application Cryptogram,&#xD;
ARPC or Secure Messaging processing. Linux/Unix and FIPS runtime verification remain&#xD;
outside the demonstrated scope; Windows tests are not cross-platform evidence.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input and Parameter Contract&#xD;
&#xD;
Use exactly three Derive arguments: PARAM, a genuine KEY reference, and PAN. Expected metadata is Secret AES.&#xD;
IMK lengths of 16, 24 and 32 bytes produce AES-128, AES-192 and AES-256 ICC Master Keys,&#xD;
respectively. Known KeySizeInBits metadata must match the actual key data.&#xD;
Conflicting declared key algorithms warn; Unknown emits no type warning. KEY literals, VAR/PARAM in the&#xD;
KEY position and value-based structured KEY lookup are not allowed. Identical values in&#xD;
different KEY variables do not override the referenced KEY's metadata.&#xD;
&#xD;
PAN must be a direct normal string literal or a referenced normal string VAR containing&#xD;
1-19 ASCII decimal digits. Leading zeros and the check digit are retained. HEX/Base64,&#xD;
integer, KEY/PARAM and nested function results are rejected. No implicit conversion,&#xD;
trimming, normalization, Luhn validation or check-digit removal takes place.&#xD;
&#xD;
Only MECH and optional PSN parameters are permitted. MECH must consistently select&#xD;
KDF-EMV-MASTER-C. PSN must be a direct normal string literal containing exactly two ASCII&#xD;
digits, for example `#PSN:"45"`; omission defaults to `"00"`. Empty, integer, variable,&#xD;
HEX/Base64 and malformed PSN values are rejected. Duplicate PSN or MECH declarations&#xD;
are rejected even if identical. A stored MECH contradicting Parameter.Mechanism is rejected.&#xD;
These rules apply to Parameters(...), direct PARAM declarations and serialized legacy PARAMs.&#xD;
Malformed serialized segments are rejected before lossy legacy parsing. The explicit legacy&#xD;
Derive(string[]) adapter retains its registered-key lookup; structured calls never fall back&#xD;
to this lookup. Other mechanisms retain their existing parameter contracts.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Derivation Algorithm&#xD;
&#xD;
Concatenate PAN || PSN and encode Packed Decimal, left-padded with zero nibbles to exactly&#xD;
16 bytes (32 digits), to form Y. No PAN truncation, SHA-1, ASCII encryption or F fill nibble.&#xD;
&#xD;
- AES-128: ICC = AES-ECB(IMK, Y), exactly one block.&#xD;
- AES-192: ICC = AES-ECB(IMK, Y) || leftmost8(AES-ECB(IMK, Y*)).&#xD;
- AES-256: ICC = AES-ECB(IMK, Y) || AES-ECB(IMK, Y*).&#xD;
&#xD;
Y* is the full bitwise complement of all 16 bytes of Y, including the left padding.&#xD;
ECB operations use no IV and no padding. No DES Odd Parity correction is applied.&#xD;
The ICC key length equals the IMK length. Option A/B use TDEA and their own preparation&#xD;
rules; Option C is never selected as an automatic fallback from either mechanism.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Executable Example and Key Hierarchy&#xD;
&#xD;
```text&#xD;
KEY IMK = GenerateKey(AES-ECB, 0x(2EF6E07ECBA86BCF3C3CFF7BBEBE6F38))&#xD;
PARAM p = Parameters(KDF-EMV-MASTER-C, #PSN:"45")&#xD;
KEY ICC = Derive(p, IMK, "99012345678901234")&#xD;
PARAM sessionParameters = Parameters(KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION)&#xD;
KEY sessionKey = Derive(sessionParameters, ICC, 0x(0001))&#xD;
```&#xD;
&#xD;
Y = 00000000000009901234567890123445.&#xD;
ICC = 35C006583E2B417207AE5B166C0EA6EF.&#xD;
Session key = 1E955EB21AFFA73F01DBED4D41BD25F4.&#xD;
&#xD;
Equivalent direct parameters: `PARAM p = #MECH:KDF-EMV-MASTER-C #PSN:"45"`.&#xD;
Default PSN: `PARAM p = Parameters(KDF-EMV-MASTER-C)`.&#xD;
PAN can be declared as `VAR pan = "99012345678901234"` and passed as `Derive(p, IMK, pan)`.&#xD;
The ICC KEY is directly usable with KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION without manual conversion.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Result Metadata and Security&#xD;
&#xD;
Secret AES; KeySizeInBits 128, 192 or 256; ValueFormat HEX; identical Value and KeyValue;&#xD;
KeyUsage Unspecified; DerivationMechanism KDF-EMV-MASTER-C. The subsequent session KEY&#xD;
retains the AES size and reports KDF-EMV-AC-SESSION. No extra key-usage or AES weak-key&#xD;
policy is introduced.&#xD;
&#xD;
Errors do not echo key, PAN or PSN values. The core uses one IMK snapshot, then only that&#xD;
snapshot for validation and AES. Owned decoded-key, snapshot, diversification and ECB&#xD;
buffers are zeroized in finally blocks, including results that are not returned on failure.&#xD;
The wrapper also clears the derived byte array after creating the CryptoScript KEY value.&#xD;
Immutable CryptoScript KEY/PAN/PSN strings cannot reliably be zeroized.&#xD;
Callers must prevent concurrent mutation while an input-key snapshot is being copied;&#xD;
the copy is not guaranteed to be atomic against external writers.&#xD;
Dynamic provider-fault injection and observation of all private buffers after exceptions&#xD;
remain test limitations; no production fault-injection abstraction is introduced.&#xD;
&#xD;
## References&#xD;
&#xD;
EMV Contact Book 2 v4.4, Annex A1.4.3 Option C, page 140; AES variants in A1.4, page 138.&#xD;
EMVCo Specification Bulletin 162, April 2015, pages 1-2 (corrects the earlier 8-byte mask).&#xD;
ICC and ATC-0001 session values were independently checked with Python cryptography / OpenSSL,&#xD;
without invoking the CryptoScript cores; they are not product-certification evidence.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: Aes. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The selected Option A/B/C determines the algorithm; conflicting KEY declarations never select another option. PAN, PSN, genuine-reference and known-size consistency checks remain binding.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM KDF-EP2-SESSION&#xD;
&#xD;
KDF-EP2-SESSION derives the session-key variants defined by ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0 section 8.11. SHA-256, each HKDF info value and each output length are fixed by the selected variant.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Complete HKDF**: The 16-byte Session Key is the IKM for HKDF-Extract-SHA256 with the required 32-byte salt. The resulting PRK is used by HKDF-Expand-SHA256.&#xD;
- **Variant Info**: #VARIANT selects a fixed info byte sequence. The info value is used directly and is not hashed.&#xD;
- **Output Length**: The selected variant fixes the result at 16 or 32 bytes. #OUTLEN is not supported.&#xD;
- **Fixed Hash**: SHA-256 is fixed by the ep2 profile. #HASH is not supported.&#xD;
- **Info Placeholder**: The third Derive argument must be present and empty because info is defined by #VARIANT.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Data Flow&#xD;
&#xD;
Session Key and salt are processed by HKDF-Extract-SHA256. HKDF-Expand-SHA256 then combines the resulting PRK with the selected variant info to produce the Session Key Variant.&#xD;
&#xD;
| Variant | Info | Output |&#xD;
|---------|------|--------|&#xD;
| TC | 7184718471847184 | 32 Byte |&#xD;
| MAC-SEND | EB5AEB5AEB5AEB5A | 32 Byte |&#xD;
| MAC-RECEIVE | 9309930993099309 | 32 Byte |&#xD;
| ENCRYPTION | 0C7E0C7E0C7E0C7E | 16 Byte |&#xD;
| PIN | C5B1C5B1C5B1C5B1 | 16 Byte |&#xD;
| KEY-ENCRYPTION | 3FE73FE73FE73FE7 | 16 Byte |&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: no algorithm restriction on raw input keying material. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The HMAC Extract key is the salt; the IKM is not required to be typed Hmac. No KeyTypeConflict is produced for this input. Hash, salt, variant and output-length contracts are unchanged.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
&#xD;
| Functions | Parameters | Input | Output | Key Length |&#xD;
|-----------|------------|-------|--------|------------|&#xD;
| Parameters | N/A | #MECH, #SALT, #VARIANT | PARAM variable | N/A |&#xD;
| Derive | #MECH, #SALT, #VARIANT | parameters, Session Key, empty data | Generic KEY variable | 128 or 256 bits |&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input Requirements&#xD;
&#xD;
- The Session Key must be exactly 16 bytes.&#xD;
- #SALT must resolve to exactly 32 bytes.&#xD;
- The third Derive argument must be the empty string (`""`); non-empty data is rejected.&#xD;
- Salt accepts `0x(...)` hexadecimal bytes, `b64(...)` decoded Base64 bytes, a UTF-8 string, or the stored byte representation of a variable.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
&#xD;
- **#MECH**: Required. Specifies KDF-EP2-SESSION.&#xD;
- **#SALT**: Required and exactly 32 bytes.&#xD;
- **#VARIANT**: Required. Accepts TC, MAC-SEND, MAC-RECEIVE, ENCRYPTION, PIN or KEY-ENCRYPTION.&#xD;
- **#HASH**: Not supported; SHA-256 is fixed.&#xD;
- **#OUTLEN**: Not supported; the variant fixes the output length.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
KEY sessionKey = GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, 0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01))&#xD;
PARAM sessionKdf = Parameters(KDF-EP2-SESSION, #SALT:0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01456789ABCDEF01236789ABCDEF012345), #VARIANT:TC)&#xD;
KEY sessionVariant = Derive(sessionKdf, sessionKey, "")&#xD;
PARAM encryptionKdf = Parameters(KDF-EP2-SESSION, #SALT:0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01456789ABCDEF01236789ABCDEF012345), #VARIANT:ENCRYPTION)&#xD;
KEY encryptionVariant = Derive(encryptionKdf, sessionKey, "")&#xD;
&#xD;
The TC example returns `CDA5C89A6B4F073779AB3B882C5CDFEFE756621E2FD4C4AC46C9FFCB9915C5CC`. The ENCRYPTION example returns `29DDFF143885D21CD2425D1FDDA2C229`.&#xD;
&#xD;
## ep2 Reference&#xD;
&#xD;
ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0, section 8.11, Session Key Variant Generation.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRX&#xD;
&#xD;
KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRX derives Key PAN Receipt Trx as defined by ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0 section 8.12.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **No Extract**: The 16-byte Key PAN Receipt Trm is used directly by HKDF-Expand-SHA256.&#xD;
- **Hashed DOL Info**: Info is SHA-256(DOL), not the raw DOL.&#xD;
- **Truncation**: Expand produces 32 bytes; the leftmost 16 bytes are returned and the rightmost 16 bytes are discarded.&#xD;
- **Fixed Profile**: SHA-256 and all output lengths are fixed. No additional parameters are supported.&#xD;
- **8.12 versus 8.14**: Section 8.12 hashes the DOL before HKDF-Expand. Section 8.14 uses the DOL directly as HKDF info.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Data Flow&#xD;
&#xD;
The complete DOL byte sequence is hashed with SHA-256. That digest is used as info for a 32-byte HKDF-Expand-SHA256 operation keyed directly by Key PAN Receipt Trm. The result is truncated to its leftmost 16 bytes.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: Hmac for the Expand PRK. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The mechanism and its explicit hash/profile determine the computation, independently of declared KEY metadata.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
&#xD;
| Functions | Parameters | Input | Output | Key Length |&#xD;
|-----------|------------|-------|--------|------------|&#xD;
| Parameters | N/A | #MECH | PARAM variable | N/A |&#xD;
| Derive | #MECH | parameters, Key PAN Receipt Trm, DOL data | Generic KEY variable | 128 bits |&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input Requirements&#xD;
&#xD;
- Key PAN Receipt Trm must be exactly 16 bytes.&#xD;
- DOL is the third Derive argument. Its length is not structurally restricted, and an empty byte sequence is valid.&#xD;
- `0x(...)` supplies raw hexadecimal bytes, `b64(...)` supplies decoded Base64 bytes, and `"..."` supplies UTF-8 bytes. A variable supplies its stored byte representation.&#xD;
- A string that looks hexadecimal remains UTF-8 text. CryptoScript does not parse the DOL structure.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
&#xD;
- **#MECH**: Required. Specifies KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRX.&#xD;
- No additional parameters are supported. SHA-256, the 32-byte expansion and the 16-byte result are fixed by ep2.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
KEY terminalKey = GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, 0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01))&#xD;
PARAM receiptTrxKdf = Parameters(KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRX)&#xD;
KEY receiptTrx = Derive(receiptTrxKdf, terminalKey, 0x(1322042006015445524D3132333412345678A000000111))&#xD;
&#xD;
The example returns `D37FF3ECC3F2EAC21EF3C968A9DE2934`.&#xD;
&#xD;
## ep2 Reference&#xD;
&#xD;
ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0, section 8.12, Key Derivation Function for Key PAN Receipt Trx.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRM&#xD;
&#xD;
KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRM derives Key PAN Receipt Trm as defined by ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0 section 8.13.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Complete HKDF**: The 16-byte Key PAN Receipt Acq and required 32-byte Salt PAN Receipt Acq are used directly by HKDF-Extract-SHA256.&#xD;
- **Hashed Properties Info**: Only Terminal Properties are hashed. Info is SHA-256(Terminal Properties); the key and salt are not pre-hashed.&#xD;
- **Truncation**: HKDF-Expand-SHA256 produces 32 bytes; the leftmost 16 bytes are returned and the rightmost 16 bytes are discarded.&#xD;
- **Fixed Profile**: SHA-256 and the output lengths are fixed. #HASH, #OUTLEN and #VARIANT are not supported.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Data Flow&#xD;
&#xD;
HKDF-Extract-SHA256 derives a PRK from Salt PAN Receipt Acq and Key PAN Receipt Acq. Separately, SHA-256 hashes the complete Terminal Properties byte sequence. HKDF-Expand-SHA256 uses the PRK and this digest as info to produce 32 bytes, which are truncated to the leftmost 16 bytes.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: no algorithm restriction on raw input keying material. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The HMAC Extract key is the salt; the IKM is not required to be typed Hmac. No KeyTypeConflict is produced for this input. Hash, salt, variant and output-length contracts are unchanged.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
&#xD;
| Functions | Parameters | Input | Output | Key Length |&#xD;
|-----------|------------|-------|--------|------------|&#xD;
| Parameters | N/A | #MECH, #SALT | PARAM variable | N/A |&#xD;
| Derive | #MECH, #SALT | parameters, Key PAN Receipt Acq, Terminal Properties | Generic KEY variable | 128 bits |&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input Requirements&#xD;
&#xD;
- Key PAN Receipt Acq must be exactly 16 bytes.&#xD;
- #SALT must resolve to exactly 32 bytes.&#xD;
- Terminal Properties are the third Derive argument. Their length is not structurally restricted, and an empty byte sequence is valid.&#xD;
- `0x(...)` supplies raw hexadecimal bytes, `b64(...)` supplies decoded Base64 bytes, and `"..."` supplies UTF-8 bytes. A variable supplies its stored byte representation.&#xD;
- A string that looks hexadecimal remains UTF-8 text. CryptoScript does not parse the Terminal Properties structure.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
&#xD;
- **#MECH**: Required. Specifies KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRM.&#xD;
- **#SALT**: Required and exactly 32 bytes.&#xD;
- **#HASH**: Not supported; SHA-256 is fixed.&#xD;
- **#OUTLEN**: Not supported; expansion and result lengths are fixed.&#xD;
- **#VARIANT**: Not supported.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
KEY acquirerKey = GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, 0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01))&#xD;
PARAM receiptTrmKdf = Parameters(KDF-EP2-PAN-RECEIPT-TRM, #SALT:0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01456789ABCDEF01236789ABCDEF012345))&#xD;
KEY receiptTrm = Derive(receiptTrmKdf, acquirerKey, 0x(5445524D313233340345ABC56876FF1900BCDEF3FCD35914))&#xD;
&#xD;
The example returns `F9B286738F4A3848C20C7CB12D3886E7`.&#xD;
&#xD;
## ep2 Reference&#xD;
&#xD;
ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0, section 8.13, Key Derivation Function for Key PAN Receipt Trm.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM KDF-EP2-PAN-SURROGATE-TRX&#xD;
&#xD;
KDF-EP2-PAN-SURROGATE-TRX derives Key PAN Surrogate Trx as defined by ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0 section 8.14.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **No Extract**: The 16-byte Merchant Key is used directly by HKDF-Expand-SHA256.&#xD;
- **Raw DOL Info**: The DOL byte sequence is used directly as HKDF info. It is not pre-hashed.&#xD;
- **Full Output**: Expand produces and returns all 32 bytes; no truncation is performed.&#xD;
- **Fixed Profile**: SHA-256 and the 32-byte output are fixed. No additional parameters are supported.&#xD;
- **Primary and Secondary**: Both use this mechanism. Their results differ through the supplied keys or data, not through separate mechanism names.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Data Flow&#xD;
&#xD;
HKDF-Expand-SHA256 uses the Merchant Key directly with the raw DOL as info and returns the complete 32-byte result. There is no Extract, DOL hash or truncation step.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Semantic diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected KeyType metadata: Hmac for the Expand PRK. Expected KeyUsage: `DeriveKey`.&#xD;
Declarative conflicts emit non-blocking `KeyTypeConflict` then `KeyUsageConflict` warnings where a fixed expected algorithm exists. Correct metadata emits no conflict warning; Unknown KeyType and Unspecified usage impose no diagnostic restriction. These checks do not mutate KEY or PARAM and work without a diagnostic sink. The mechanism and its explicit hash/profile determine the computation, independently of declared KEY metadata.&#xD;
&#xD;
Malformed key bytes, unsupported key lengths, invalid parameters and mandatory protocol or authentication checks remain blocking. Warnings do not establish PCI or EP2 conformity; the EP2 security matrix remains open.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
&#xD;
| Functions | Parameters | Input | Output | Key Length |&#xD;
|-----------|------------|-------|--------|------------|&#xD;
| Parameters | N/A | #MECH | PARAM variable | N/A |&#xD;
| Derive | #MECH | parameters, Merchant Key, DOL data | Generic KEY variable | 256 bits |&#xD;
&#xD;
## Input Requirements&#xD;
&#xD;
- The Merchant Key must be exactly 16 bytes.&#xD;
- DOL is the third Derive argument. Its length is not structurally restricted, and an empty byte sequence is valid.&#xD;
- `0x(...)` supplies raw hexadecimal bytes, `b64(...)` supplies decoded Base64 bytes, and `"..."` supplies UTF-8 bytes. A variable supplies its stored byte representation.&#xD;
- A string that looks hexadecimal remains UTF-8 text. CryptoScript does not parse the DOL structure.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
&#xD;
- **#MECH**: Required. Specifies KDF-EP2-PAN-SURROGATE-TRX.&#xD;
- No additional parameters are supported. SHA-256 and the 32-byte result are fixed by ep2.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
KEY merchantKey = GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, 0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01))&#xD;
PARAM surrogateTrxKdf = Parameters(KDF-EP2-PAN-SURROGATE-TRX)&#xD;
KEY surrogateTrx = Derive(surrogateTrxKdf, merchantKey, 0x(5413330089020011))&#xD;
&#xD;
The example returns `AEA780CDFC3CDA67C0FD0D70D509C9B4C1DD1F40F0C05D922BFD3BC8A01E2E6E`.&#xD;
&#xD;
## ep2 Reference&#xD;
&#xD;
ep2 Security Specification V8.3.0, section 8.14, Key Derivation Function for Key PAN Surrogate Trx.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM EP2-MESSAGE-MAC&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
Computes `Left8(HMAC-SHA256(K, SHA256(messageBytes)))` according to EP2 Security Specification 8.3.0,&#xD;
section 5.3.1 (pages 9-36 and 9-37), and section 8.2 (page 9-57).&#xD;
`Left8` takes the leftmost eight bytes of the complete HMAC-SHA256 output.&#xD;
The return value is a VAR containing exactly eight bytes in `0x(...)` HEX format.&#xD;
There is no configurable digest, padding, IV or output length.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Input boundary&#xD;
&#xD;
The caller supplies the unchanged bytes of the data stream after the MAC field. The MAC field is excluded. No XML normalization, reserialization, encoding changes or whitespace changes are performed. The inner SHA-256 output is 32 binary bytes, never hexadecimal text. Error Notification messages have no MAC; that protocol decision belongs to the caller.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Keys and diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Supply exactly 32 bytes in a genuine KEY reference. Known key size metadata must match&#xD;
256 bits. Import with GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, ...) or separately derive the session&#xD;
key variant using KDF-EP2-SESSION (sections 5.1.4 and 8.11).&#xD;
Use MAC-SEND for messages from the master; MAC-RECEIVE for messages to the master.&#xD;
The raw 16-byte Session Key is not a MAC key. Variant selection belongs to the caller;&#xD;
DerivationMechanism alone does not prove the selected variant.&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected metadata is Hmac and MacGenerate. KeyAlgorithm.Unknown and unspecified usage&#xD;
are accepted. Conflicting declared algorithm/usage produces non-blocking warnings,&#xD;
without modifying the KEY. Invalid bytes, key sizes and argument contracts block execution.&#xD;
These mechanisms perform calculation only; they do not verify a received MAC or enforce&#xD;
protocol validity. Section 5.3.2 requires rejection on verification failure at the protocol layer.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
&#xD;
| Function | Input | Output |&#xD;
|---|---|---|&#xD;
| Parameters | EP2-MESSAGE-MAC, no additional arguments | PARAM |&#xD;
| Mac | PARAM reference, KEY reference, HEX data | Eight-byte HEX VAR |&#xD;
&#xD;
GenerateKey, Hash, Derive, Encrypt, Decrypt, Wrap and Unwrap are not supported by this&#xD;
mechanism. Use the existing HMAC or KDF mechanisms to prepare keys.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
&#xD;
Only `#MECH:EP2-MESSAGE-MAC` is supported. Additional and duplicate parameters are forbidden.&#xD;
Both Parameters(...) and direct `PARAM p = #MECH:EP2-MESSAGE-MAC` declarations are supported.&#xD;
&#xD;
`Mac(p, k, data)` requires exactly three positional arguments:&#xD;
&#xD;
- Position 0: genuine PARAM variable reference with the matching MECH.&#xD;
- Position 1: genuine KEY variable reference; no raw key literals or VAR substitutes.&#xD;
- Position 2: direct HEX literal or genuine HEX VAR reference.&#xD;
&#xD;
Reconstructed PARAM/KEY objects, serialized values, nested function results, strings,&#xD;
Base64 and implicit conversions are rejected. Identical key bytes do not merge object identity.&#xD;
Legacy Mac(string[]) is explicitly unsupported for these mechanisms.&#xD;
The cryptographic core permits empty bytes, without claiming that an empty message or&#xD;
transaction is EP2-valid. The current grammar cannot spell an empty HEX literal `0x()`;&#xD;
no grammar extension or string-input exception is introduced here.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
The following official example uses the same key and data as section 5.3.1 (pages 9-36 and 9-37).&#xD;
The expected result is `0x(96B632C95451623B)`.&#xD;
&#xD;
```cryptoscript&#xD;
KEY k = GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, 0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01456789ABCDEF01236789ABCDEF012345))&#xD;
PARAM p = Parameters(EP2-MESSAGE-MAC)&#xD;
VAR data = 0x(11112222333344445555)&#xD;
VAR result = Mac(p, k, data)&#xD;
```&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM EP2-TRANSACTION-CERTIFICATE&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
Computes `Left8(HMAC-SHA256(K, preparedDolBytes))` according to EP2 Security Specification 8.3.0,&#xD;
section 5.6 (page 9-41), and section 8.2 (page 9-57).&#xD;
`Left8` takes the leftmost eight bytes of the complete HMAC-SHA256 output.&#xD;
The return value is a VAR containing exactly eight bytes in `0x(...)` HEX format.&#xD;
There is no configurable digest, padding, IV or output length.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Input boundary&#xD;
&#xD;
The caller supplies the already prepared binary DOL value sequence. There is no additional SHA-256 prehash, DOL parser or TLV processing. DOL selection, object lookup, ordering and length handling belong to the caller. Use plaintext values for fields that will themselves be encrypted (for example PAN). Treat date, expdate and time as numeric (n). General DOL handling follows EMV 4.3 Book 3 section 5.4. Select the application DOL, or the Reservation/FollowOn DOL for the transaction types specified in the footnote to section 5.6. The MAC cannot validate these preparation rules from the resulting bytes.&#xD;
&#xD;
### Keys and diagnostics&#xD;
&#xD;
Supply exactly 32 bytes in a genuine KEY reference. Known key size metadata must match&#xD;
256 bits. Import with GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, ...) or separately derive the session&#xD;
key variant using KDF-EP2-SESSION (sections 5.1.4 and 8.11).&#xD;
Use TC for the Acquirer Transaction Certificate.&#xD;
The raw 16-byte Session Key is not a MAC key. Variant selection belongs to the caller;&#xD;
DerivationMechanism alone does not prove the selected variant.&#xD;
&#xD;
Expected metadata is Hmac and MacGenerate. KeyAlgorithm.Unknown and unspecified usage&#xD;
are accepted. Conflicting declared algorithm/usage produces non-blocking warnings,&#xD;
without modifying the KEY. Invalid bytes, key sizes and argument contracts block execution.&#xD;
These mechanisms perform calculation only; they do not verify a received MAC or enforce&#xD;
protocol validity. Section 5.3.2 requires rejection on verification failure at the protocol layer.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
&#xD;
| Function | Input | Output |&#xD;
|---|---|---|&#xD;
| Parameters | EP2-TRANSACTION-CERTIFICATE, no additional arguments | PARAM |&#xD;
| Mac | PARAM reference, KEY reference, HEX data | Eight-byte HEX VAR |&#xD;
&#xD;
GenerateKey, Hash, Derive, Encrypt, Decrypt, Wrap and Unwrap are not supported by this&#xD;
mechanism. Use the existing HMAC or KDF mechanisms to prepare keys.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
&#xD;
Only `#MECH:EP2-TRANSACTION-CERTIFICATE` is supported. Additional and duplicate parameters are forbidden.&#xD;
Both Parameters(...) and direct `PARAM p = #MECH:EP2-TRANSACTION-CERTIFICATE` declarations are supported.&#xD;
&#xD;
`Mac(p, k, data)` requires exactly three positional arguments:&#xD;
&#xD;
- Position 0: genuine PARAM variable reference with the matching MECH.&#xD;
- Position 1: genuine KEY variable reference; no raw key literals or VAR substitutes.&#xD;
- Position 2: direct HEX literal or genuine HEX VAR reference.&#xD;
&#xD;
Reconstructed PARAM/KEY objects, serialized values, nested function results, strings,&#xD;
Base64 and implicit conversions are rejected. Identical key bytes do not merge object identity.&#xD;
Legacy Mac(string[]) is explicitly unsupported for these mechanisms.&#xD;
The cryptographic core permits empty bytes, without claiming that an empty message or&#xD;
transaction is EP2-valid. The current grammar cannot spell an empty HEX literal `0x()`;&#xD;
no grammar extension or string-input exception is introduced here.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
&#xD;
The following official example uses the same key and data as section 5.6 (page 9-41).&#xD;
The expected result is `0x(6FD9A4429C6348AA)`.&#xD;
&#xD;
```cryptoscript&#xD;
KEY k = GenerateKey(HMAC-SHA256, 0x(0123456789ABCDEF23456789ABCDEF01456789ABCDEF01236789ABCDEF012345))&#xD;
PARAM p = Parameters(EP2-TRANSACTION-CERTIFICATE)&#xD;
VAR data = 0x(11112222333344445555)&#xD;
VAR result = Mac(p, k, data)&#xD;
```&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM HASH-SHA1&#xD;
&#xD;
HASH-SHA1 calculates an unkeyed message digest using the SHA-1 hash function. It supports hashing rather than message authentication, encryption or decryption.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Hash Function**: SHA-1 produces a 160-bit digest and is specified in FIPS 180-4.&#xD;
- **Security Note**: SHA-1 is not recommended for new applications. Migration to a SHA-2 or SHA-3 mechanism is recommended, while CryptoScript continues to support HASH-SHA1.&#xD;
- **Input Data**: Hash accepts hexadecimal data, Base64 data, UTF-8 string data, or data stored in a VAR. Empty input is accepted.&#xD;
- **Digest Length**: Hash always returns the complete 20-byte SHA-1 digest. The output length is not configurable.&#xD;
- **Return Value**: Hash returns a VAR containing hexadecimal digest bytes in 0x(...) format.&#xD;
- **Keys**: HASH-SHA1 is unkeyed. GenerateKey is not supported.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
The following functions are available for HASH-SHA1 mechanism:&#xD;
&#xD;
| Functions | Parameters | Input | Output | Key Length |&#xD;
|------------|-------------------|-------------------------------------|----------------|------------------|&#xD;
| Parameters | N/A | #MECH | PARAM variable | N/A |&#xD;
| Hash | #MECH | parameters, data | VAR digest | N/A |&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
These parameters are used with the HASH-SHA1 mechanism:&#xD;
- **#MECH**: Specifies HASH-SHA1 in PARAM.&#xD;
- **Additional Parameters**: Parameters rejects additional parameters.&#xD;
- **#IV**: Not supported.&#xD;
- **#PAD**: Not supported.&#xD;
- **#MACLEN**: Not supported.&#xD;
- **Salt and Output Length**: Not supported. The result always contains the complete 20-byte digest.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
### HASH-SHA1 Digest Calculation&#xD;
PARAM p0       = Parameters(HASH-SHA1)  &#xD;
VAR cleartext  = "This is a secret message."  &#xD;
VAR digest     = Hash(p0, cleartext)  &#xD;
### HASH-SHA1 Empty Input&#xD;
VAR emptyhash  = Hash(p0, "")  &#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM HASH-SHA224&#xD;
&#xD;
HASH-SHA224 calculates an unkeyed message digest using the SHA-224 hash function. It supports hashing rather than message authentication, encryption or decryption.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Hash Function**: SHA-224 belongs to the SHA-2 family, produces a 224-bit digest and is specified in FIPS 180-4.&#xD;
- **Input Data**: Hash accepts hexadecimal data, Base64 data, UTF-8 string data, or data stored in a VAR. Empty input is accepted.&#xD;
- **Digest Length**: Hash always returns the complete 28-byte SHA-224 digest. The output length is not configurable.&#xD;
- **Return Value**: Hash returns a VAR containing hexadecimal digest bytes in 0x(...) format.&#xD;
- **Keys**: HASH-SHA224 is unkeyed. GenerateKey is not supported.&#xD;
&#xD;
## Functions&#xD;
The following functions are available for HASH-SHA224 mechanism:&#xD;
&#xD;
| Functions | Parameters | Input | Output | Key Length |&#xD;
|------------|-------------------|-------------------------------------|----------------|------------------|&#xD;
| Parameters | N/A | #MECH | PARAM variable | N/A |&#xD;
| Hash | #MECH | parameters, data | VAR digest | N/A |&#xD;
&#xD;
## Parameters&#xD;
These parameters are used with the HASH-SHA224 mechanism:&#xD;
- **#MECH**: Specifies HASH-SHA224 in PARAM.&#xD;
- **Additional Parameters**: Parameters rejects additional parameters.&#xD;
- **#IV**: Not supported.&#xD;
- **#PAD**: Not supported.&#xD;
- **#MACLEN**: Not supported.&#xD;
- **Salt and Output Length**: Not supported. The result always contains the complete 28-byte digest.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
## Example Usage&#xD;
### HASH-SHA224 Digest Calculation&#xD;
PARAM p0       = Parameters(HASH-SHA224)  &#xD;
VAR cleartext  = "This is a secret message."  &#xD;
VAR digest     = Hash(p0, cleartext)  &#xD;
### HASH-SHA224 Empty Input&#xD;
VAR emptyhash  = Hash(p0, "")  &#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
&#xD;
# MECHANISM HASH-SHA256&#xD;
&#xD;
HASH-SHA256 calculates an unkeyed message digest using the SHA-256 hash function. It supports hashing rather than message authentication, encryption or decryption.&#xD;
&#xD;
---&#xD;
&#xD;
## Key Features&#xD;
&#xD;
- **Hash Function**: SHA-256 belongs to the SHA-2 family, produces a 256-bit digest and is specified in FIPS 180-4.&#xD;
- **Input Data**: Hash accepts hexadecimal data, Base64 data, UTF-8 string data, or data stored in a VAR. Empty input is accepted.&#xD;
- **Digest Length**: Hash always returns the complete 32-byte SHA-256 digest. The output length is not configurable.&#xD;
- **Return Value**: Hash returns a VAR containing hexadecimal digest bytes in 0x(...) format.&#xD;
